aza: PSA: found a cancellation leak in the onboarding sample when using Retrofit -> suspend wrappers. Navigating away cancels the coroutine but the underlying OkHttp Call keeps running (uploads/streams continue). Causes extra bandwidth and occasional duplicate requests on retry. Anyone seen this?

mike: Yep, common pattern: developers wrap Call.enqueue or Call.execute in suspendCoroutine but forget to tie Job cancellation to call.cancel(). Without that the call keeps working even if coroutine dies.

aza: Minimal problematic helper from sample:
```kotlin
suspend fun <T> Call<T>.awaitResponse(): Response<T> = suspendCoroutine { cont ->
  enqueue(object: Callback<T> {
    override fun onResponse(call: Call<T>, response: Response<T>) = cont.resume(response)
    override fun onFailure(call: Call<T>, t: Throwable) = cont.resumeWithException(t)
  })
}
```

aza: When the coroutine is cancelled this doesn't cancel the OkHttp Call. On Android navigating back often cancels the coroutine which should cancel network work too.

lee: Fix is small: register invokeOnCancellation and call.cancel(). Also consider using suspendCancellableCoroutine which has built-in cancellation support. Example fix below.

mike: Snippet we added to SDK-samples:
```kotlin
suspend fun <T> Call<T>.awaitCancellable(): Response<T> = suspendCancellableCoroutine { cont ->
  cont.invokeOnCancellation { try { cancel() } catch (_: Throwable) {} }
  enqueue(object: Callback<T> {
    override fun onResponse(call: Call<T>, response: Response<T>) { if (!cont.isCancelled) cont.resume(response) }
    override fun onFailure(call: Call<T>, t: Throwable) { if (!cont.isCancelled) cont.resumeWithException(t) }
  })
}
```

lee: Also note: if you do heavy work in onResponse you should check cont.isActive/cont.isCancelled to avoid races.

aza: I updated sample-app and added a tiny e2e that navigates away during a streaming upload and asserts server only saw one request. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sample-app/pull/201 (draft).

